Absolutely — if we treat “literature” broadly enough to include novels, folktales, manga, and literary-inspired works, there are enough distinct tanuki characters to make a meaningful top 20. I’d rank them roughly by recognition, cultural impact, and prominence in their work.

#	Tanuki character	Work	Personality
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

1	Shukaku	= Naruto =	A volatile, arrogant, and ultimately tragic creature whose hostility hides a deep loneliness.

2	Bunta	= Pom Poko	= A thoughtful, courageous young tanuki who becomes a leader in the fight to protect his home.

3	Bunbuku Chagama	= The Tale of Bunbuku Chagama	= A grateful, playful shapeshifter whose magical antics reward kindness.

4	Kachi-kachi Yama Tanuki	Kachi-kachi Yama	= A cruel, murderous trickster whose viciousness makes him one of Japanese folklore's classic villains.

5	Yasaburō Shimogamo = The Eccentric Family (Uchōten Kazoku)	= A carefree, philosophical, and mischievous tanuki who prefers enjoying life to taking responsibility.

6	Yajirō Shimogamo	= The Eccentric Family	= A timid, melancholy tanuki who retreats from the world after a traumatic experience.

7	Yaichirō Shimogamo	= The Eccentric Family	= Earnest and responsible, he desperately wants to live up to his father's legacy.

8	Yasaburō's father, Souichirō Shimogamo	= The Eccentric Family	= Charismatic and respected, he was a warm family man whose disappearance casts a shadow over the story.

9	Hachirō Shimogamo	= The Eccentric Family	= A gentle, easygoing tanuki who largely avoids conflict and enjoys a quiet life.

10	Bunbuku's Tanuki	Japanese Fairy Tales  Bunbuku Chagama tradition = Cheeky and theatrical, he uses shapeshifting to entertain and repay the kindness of his human companion.

11	Tanuki-bayashi tanuki	Shōjōji no Tanuki-bayashi	= Mischievous and exuberant spirits who lure people into the night with supernatural drumming and dancing.

12	Tanuki of Tono Monogatari =	Tono Monogatari	= Eerie and unpredictable shape-shifters representing the unsettling boundary between ordinary rural life and the supernatural.

13	Tanuki of Kachi-kachi Yama retellings	Japanese folktale tradition =	Cunning and vindictive, they embody the dangerous side of the traditional trickster tanuki.

14	Tanuki in Konjaku Monogatarishū	Konjaku Monogatarishū	= Often sly and opportunistic, they use transformation and deception to meddle in human affairs.

15	Tanuki in Uji Shūi Monogatari	Uji Shūi Monogatari	= Comical and troublesome supernatural creatures whose schemes frequently backfire.

16	Tanuki in Otogizōshi	= Otogizōshi	= Shapeshifting tricksters who blur the line between the human and supernatural worlds.
